<div class="sim-root">
  <div class="sim-main">
    <div class="rl-tabs">
      <button type="button" class="rl-tabbtn active" data-tab="simulator">Simulator</button>
      <button type="button" class="rl-tabbtn" data-tab="article">Article</button>
    </div>

    <div class="rl-tab" data-tab="simulator">
      <div class="panel">
        <h2>Descent camera</h2>
        <canvas id="scene"></canvas>
      </div>

      <div class="controls">
        <div class="group">
          <span class="tag">throttle</span>
          <div class="ctl">
            <input id="throttle" type="range" min="0" max="100" value="0" />
            <span class="val" id="throttleV">0%</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <button id="reset">Reset run</button>
      </div>

      <div class="hud">
        <span>altitude <b id="alt">900</b> m</span>
        <span>vertical speed <b id="vspeed">-85.0</b> m/s</span>
        <span>fuel <b id="fuel">100</b>%</span>
        <span>status <b id="phase">Falling</b></span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="rl-tab" data-tab="article" hidden>
      <article class="rl-article">
        <section class="rl-section">
          <div class="rl-body">
            <h2><span class="rl-num">00</span>The shape of the problem</h2>
            <p>
              A booster returning to a pad is a rigid body with one force it can point
              and throttle, an atmosphere that pushes back, and a mass that shrinks as
              it works. It has to arrive at a specific point on the ground with almost
              no velocity, almost no tilt, and almost no rotation, using less
              propellant than it has, and it gets exactly one attempt.
            </p>
            <p>
              Four things make it genuinely hard, and they are worth naming before any
              equations appear, because every design decision later is a response to
              one of them.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>The vehicle cannot hover.</strong> At the end of the flight it is
              nearly empty, and the engine has a minimum throttle. If minimum thrust divided
              by mass exceeds local gravity, then any lit engine accelerates the vehicle
              upward. There is no steady state to settle into. You get one continuous braking
              maneuver, and it has to end at the ground.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Propellant is the binding constraint.</strong> The fuel-optimal solution
              is to fall as long as possible and brake as hard as possible at the last moment.
              That solution is also the one with the least margin, because braking late
              means every error has to be corrected with the authority left over from an
              already saturated engine.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Translation and rotation are coupled through the same actuator.</strong>
              The only way to accelerate sideways is to tilt the whole vehicle, and the
              only way to tilt is to point the engine off-axis, which steals thrust from
              the vertical channel. Wanting to move left and wanting to slow down are the
              same actuator fighting itself.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Control authority is not constant.</strong> It depends on thrust,
              air density and speed, all of which change by orders of magnitude during the
              descent. A controller tuned for the conditions at three kilometres is tuned
              for a different vehicle than the one that exists at twenty metres.
            </p>
          </div>
          <aside class="rl-rail">
            <span class="rl-rail-label">Notation used throughout</span>
            <dl class="rl-dl">
              <dt>r</dt><dd>position of the centre of mass</dd>
              <dt>v</dt><dd>velocity, inertial frame</dd>
              <dt>&phi;, &psi;</dt><dd>tilt about two horizontal axes</dd>
              <dt>&omega;</dt><dd>angular rate</dd>
              <dt>m</dt><dd>total mass, time varying</dd>
              <dt>T</dt><dd>thrust magnitude</dd>
              <dt>&delta;</dt><dd>actuator deflection</dd>
              <dt>q</dt><dd>dynamic pressure</dd>
              <dt>h</dt><dd>altitude of the engine bell</dd>
              <dt>t<sub>go</sub></dt><dd>time to go</dd>
            </dl>
            <p class="rl-note">The simulator tab is a work in progress -- these numbers are hand-computed from the vehicle's own constants, not pulled from a running model yet. Wiring the simulator up to produce them live is next.</p>
            <span class="rl-rail-label">Further reading</span>
            <ul class="rl-refs">
              <li>Blackmore, L., "Autonomous Precision Landing of Space Rockets," <em>The Bridge</em> (National Academy of Engineering), 46(4), 2016.</li>
            </ul>
          </aside>
        </section>

        <section class="rl-section">
          <div class="rl-body">
            <h2><span class="rl-num">01</span>Kinematics: choosing what to track</h2>
            <p>
              Kinematics is the bookkeeping of motion without asking what causes it. It
              sounds like the easy part. It is where most of the subtle mistakes live,
              because kinematics is where you choose what the numbers <em>mean</em>, and
              a wrong choice there is invisible until it produces a landing that is off
              by fifteen metres for no apparent reason.
            </p>

            <h3>The state vector</h3>
            <p>Pick the smallest set of numbers that determines the future. For a booster with a single centred engine:</p>
            <div class="rl-eq">x = [ rx, ry, rz,  vx, vy, vz,  &phi;, &psi;,  &omega;_&phi;, &omega;_&psi;,  m_prop ]</div>
            <p class="rl-eqcap">Eleven numbers. Everything the vehicle knows about itself.</p>
            <p>
              Three position, three velocity, two attitude angles, two angular rates, one
              propellant mass. Notice what is missing: there is no roll angle and no roll rate.
            </p>

            <h3>Why two attitude angles and not three</h3>
            <p>
              A single engine on the centreline produces a force through the centreline.
              Rotate the vehicle about its own long axis and nothing about that force
              changes. The engine has no lever arm to produce roll torque, so roll is
              uncontrollable with this actuator, and worse, it is unobservable in the
              dynamics that matter. Real boosters solve roll separately with cold gas
              thrusters or differential fin deflection, which is a different subsystem
              with a different bandwidth.
            </p>
            <p>
              Dropping it is not laziness. Carrying an unactuated third angle would let the
              vehicle drift into orientations where the two remaining angles stop being
              independent, which is gimbal lock: a coordinate singularity where two of your
              three rotation parameters describe the same physical rotation and the
              equations lose a degree of freedom. Two angles about two fixed horizontal axes
              cannot lock, and for a vehicle that never intentionally tilts past about thirty
              degrees they are exact enough.
            </p>
            <p>
              If you did need full three-axis attitude, the standard answer is quaternions,
              which have no singularities at the cost of one redundant parameter and a
              normalisation constraint you must enforce every step.
            </p>

            <h3>The body axis as a unit vector</h3>
            <p>Both angles collapse into one useful object, the direction the vehicle is pointing:</p>
            <div class="rl-eq">&ucirc;(&phi;, &psi;) = ( &minus;sin &phi;,  cos &phi; &middot; cos &psi;,  cos &phi; &middot; sin &psi; )</div>
            <p class="rl-eqcap">Thrust acts along +&ucirc;. Everything else is bookkeeping around this vector.</p>

            <div class="rl-limit">
              <span class="rl-limit-tag">The mistake worth making once</span>
              <p>The state tracks the <em>centre of mass</em>, but the vehicle touches the ground with its <em>engine bell</em>, which sits roughly sixteen metres below the centre of mass along the body axis. Altitude is therefore not <code>ry</code>:</p>
              <div class="rl-eq">h = ry &minus; L_arm &middot; &ucirc;_y = ry &minus; L_arm &middot; cos &phi; &middot; cos &psi;</div>
              <p>Tilting the vehicle <em>raises</em> its lowest point, because the bell swings up on its arm. A booster leaning ten degrees has about twenty-five centimetres more clearance than the same booster upright. Get this wrong and every altitude-triggered event fires at the wrong moment, in a way that scales with tilt, which is precisely when you can least afford it.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <aside class="rl-rail">
            <span class="rl-rail-label">Why the state is the design</span>
            <p class="rl-note">Every controller you write can only regulate quantities that exist in the state. If a physical effect is not represented, no amount of gain tuning will handle it. Adding a state later means revisiting every equation that touches it.</p>
            <span class="rl-rail-label">Frames</span>
            <dl class="rl-dl">
              <dt>Inertial</dt><dd>origin at the pad, +y up. Position, velocity and gravity live here.</dd>
              <dt>Body</dt><dd>fixed to the vehicle, +y along the nose. Thrust and fin deflections live here.</dd>
              <dt>Wind</dt><dd>aligned with airflow relative to the body. Aerodynamic forces live here.</dd>
            </dl>
            <p class="rl-note">Most integration bugs in flight software are a quantity expressed in one frame and used in another. Label your variables with their frame and the class of bug disappears.</p>
            <span class="rl-rail-label">Further reading</span>
            <ul class="rl-refs">
              <li>Hughes, P. C., <em>Spacecraft Attitude Dynamics</em>, Wiley, 1986 (Dover reprint, 2004).</li>
              <li>Shuster, M. D., "A Survey of Attitude Representations," <em>Journal of the Astronautical Sciences</em>, 41(4), 1993.</li>
            </ul>
          </aside>
        </section>

        <section class="rl-section">
          <div class="rl-body">
            <h2><span class="rl-num">02</span>Translational dynamics</h2>
            <p>Now the causes. Three forces act on the centre of mass: thrust along the deflected body axis, gravity down, drag opposing motion through the air.</p>
            <div class="rl-eq">m &middot; dv/dt = T &middot; &ucirc;(&phi; + &delta;_&phi;, &psi; + &delta;_&psi;)  &minus;  m&middot;g&middot;&yacute;  &minus;  &frac12; &rho;(h) &middot; |v_rel| &middot; v_rel &middot; C_d &middot; A</div>
            <p class="rl-eqcap">Drag is written with |v|&middot;v rather than v&sup2; so the sign follows the direction automatically in all three components.</p>
            <p>Two details in that line carry real weight.</p>
            <p>
              First, thrust points along the <em>deflected</em> axis,
              <code>&ucirc;(&phi; + &delta;_&phi;, &psi; + &delta;_&psi;)</code>, not the body axis.
              The gimbal angle is small, at most fifteen degrees, but it is the entire
              steering mechanism, so it belongs in the force equation and not just the
              torque equation.
            </p>
            <p>
              Second, the relative velocity <code>v_rel = v &minus; v_wind</code> is what
              generates drag, not ground-relative velocity. A vehicle descending vertically
              in a crosswind is flying at an angle of attack and feels a sideways force,
              which is how wind actually disturbs a booster: not by pushing on it directly,
              but by changing the direction of the air it is moving through.
            </p>

            <h3>Air density is not a constant</h3>
            <div class="rl-eq">&rho;(h) = &rho;&#8320; &middot; exp( &minus;h / H )        H &asymp; 8500 m on Earth</div>
            <p class="rl-eqcap">Scale height H is the altitude over which density falls by a factor of e.</p>
            <p>
              An exponential atmosphere is the simplest model that gets the physics right,
              and the consequence is stark. At eight kilometres the density is about a
              third of sea level. Every aerodynamic quantity in this article, drag, fin
              authority, damping, weathervane stiffness, is proportional to &rho;, so all
              of them vary by a factor of three over a stretch of descent that takes under
              a minute.
            </p>

            <h3>Mass is a state, not a parameter</h3>
            <div class="rl-eq">dm/dt = &minus;T / (I_sp &middot; g&#8320;)</div>
            <p class="rl-eqcap">Integrated over a burn, this is the Tsiolkovsky rocket equation.</p>
            <p>
              Specific impulse <code>I_sp</code> is exhaust velocity expressed in seconds,
              a convention that survives because it is unit-system independent. The vehicle
              in the header carries 5 200 kg of propellant on a 22 200 kg dry mass, so it
              burns about nineteen percent of its total mass during a landing.
            </p>
            <p>
              This matters more than it first appears. Available acceleration is
              <code>T/m</code>, and as mass falls, the same throttle setting produces more
              acceleration. A controller that is well behaved at the start of the burn is
              more aggressive at the end of it, and a pure feedforward plan computed at
              ignition will overshoot. The mass state is what lets the guidance law
              recompute against the vehicle that actually exists right now.
            </p>

            <div class="rl-limit">
              <span class="rl-limit-tag">Hard limit: the throttle floor</span>
              <p>The engine cannot run below about forty percent. The set of achievable thrust magnitudes is <code>{0} &cup; [T_min, T_max]</code>, which is <em>not convex</em>, and that single fact is why powered descent guidance is a research area rather than a textbook exercise.</p>
              <p>Concretely: near the end of the burn the vehicle masses about 24 000 kg, so minimum thrust gives 338 kN, or 14.1 m/s&sup2; of acceleration against 9.81 m/s&sup2; of gravity. A lit engine cannot descend gently. It cannot descend at all. The only options are shut down and accelerate downward at g, or light up and accelerate upward at 4.3 m/s&sup2;. Landing softly means threading between two unstable behaviours.</p>
              <p>The modern treatment is <em>lossless convexification</em> (A&ccedil;&#305;kme&#351;e and Ploen, 2007), which introduces a slack variable for thrust magnitude, relaxes the non-convex constraint to a convex one, and then proves the relaxation is tight, meaning the optimal solution of the easy problem is the optimal solution of the hard one. It is one of the genuinely elegant results in the field and it is what made real-time onboard trajectory optimisation practical.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <aside class="rl-rail">
            <span class="rl-rail-label">Orders of magnitude</span>
            <table class="rl-table">
              <tr><th>Quantity</th><th>Value</th></tr>
              <tr><td>Weight at touchdown</td><td class="n">236 kN</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Thrust at 40 %</td><td class="n">338 kN</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Thrust at 100 %</td><td class="n">845 kN</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Max deceleration</td><td class="n">3.6 g</td></tr>
              <tr><td>&Delta;v in tanks</td><td class="n">&asymp; 610 m/s</td></tr>
            </table>
            <p class="rl-note">Thrust-to-weight above one at minimum throttle is the defining feature of this vehicle. Design a lander with T/W below one at minimum throttle, as the Apollo LM had, and almost every hard problem in this article softens.</p>
            <span class="rl-rail-label">Further reading</span>
            <ul class="rl-refs">
              <li>Tsiolkovsky, K. E., <em>Исследование мировых пространств реактивными приборами</em>, 1903 -- the original rocket equation.</li>
              <li>Sutton, G. P. &amp; Biblarz, O., <em>Rocket Propulsion Elements</em>, 9th ed., Wiley, 2016.</li>
              <li><em>U.S. Standard Atmosphere, 1976</em>, NOAA/NASA/USAF -- source of the exponential density model.</li>
              <li>A&ccedil;&#305;kme&#351;e, B. &amp; Ploen, S. R., "Convex Programming Approach to Powered Descent Guidance for Mars Landing," <em>Journal of Guidance, Control, and Dynamics</em>, 30(5), 2007.</li>
              <li>A&ccedil;&#305;kme&#351;e, B., Carson, J. M. &amp; Blackmore, L., "Lossless Convexification of Nonconvex Control Bound and Pointing Constraints...," <em>IEEE Trans. Control Systems Technology</em>, 21(6), 2013.</li>
            </ul>
          </aside>
        </section>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<script>
  import * as THREE from "three";

  var canvas = document.getElementById("scene");
  var throttleInput = document.getElementById("throttle");
  var throttleLabel = document.getElementById("throttleV");
  var resetBtn = document.getElementById("reset");
  var altEl = document.getElementById("alt");
  var vspeedEl = document.getElementById("vspeed");
  var fuelEl = document.getElementById("fuel");
  var phaseEl = document.getElementById("phase");

  var renderer = new THREE.WebGLRenderer({ canvas: canvas, antialias: true });
  renderer.setPixelRatio(Math.min(window.devicePixelRatio || 1, 2));

  var scene = new THREE.Scene();
  scene.background = new THREE.Color(0x05070c);
  scene.fog = new THREE.Fog(0x05070c, 400, 2600);

  var camera = new THREE.PerspectiveCamera(50, 16 / 10, 0.5, 20000);

  // Camera rig -- a fixed chase-style framing around the pad for now (no
  // rocket telemetry to follow yet), with a slow auto-orbit so the empty
  // pad doesn't feel static. Once the vehicle exists, `camTarget` becomes
  // its position each frame and this same spherical math follows it.
  var camTarget = new THREE.Vector3(0, 20, 0);
  var camRig = { theta: -0.6, phi: 0.3, dist: 130, spin: 0 };
  var reducedMotion = window.matchMedia && window.matchMedia("(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)").matches;

  // Vehicle constants, ported from the article's own numbers (dry mass,
  // propellant, thrust, gimbal limits) -- the same values cited in the
  // Article tab's "orders of magnitude" table. Only the dimensions used
  // to place geometry are needed yet; g0/isp/Cd etc. wait for the physics
  // integration.
  var VEH = { dry: 22200, fuel0: 5200, maxT: 845000, minThr: 0.4, L: 47, R: 1.85, arm: 16 };

  // Physics constants (Article 02: exponential atmosphere, mass-flow from
  // I_sp) and the run state. 1-DOF vertical fall only -- no lateral drift,
  // gimbal or tilt yet, so drag and gravity act straight up the y axis.
  // Semi-implicit Euler at a fixed sub-step; swapping in RK4 (Article 10)
  // is a listed follow-up, not required for a stable vertical fall.
  var PHYS = { g0: 9.80665, isp: 282, rho0: 1.225, H: 8500, Cd: 0.82, A: Math.PI * VEH.R * VEH.R, vTouch: 6, step: 0.02 };
  var state = { alt: 900, vy: -85, fuel: VEH.fuel0, phase: "falling" }; // falling | landed | crashed

  var rocket, nozzleGroup, flame, flameInner, glow;
  // Leg-feet clearance at rest (see buildRocket()) -- the offset between
  // the rocket group's origin (the CoM) and "touching the pad".
  var restY = VEH.arm - 2.2 + 11;

  function buildLights() {
    var hemi = new THREE.HemisphereLight(0x8fa3b3, 0x14110c, 0.7);
    scene.add(hemi);
    var sun = new THREE.DirectionalLight(0xfff2e0, 1.1);
    sun.position.set(-300, 420, 260);
    scene.add(sun);
  }

  function buildGround() {
    var ground = new THREE.Mesh(
      new THREE.PlaneGeometry(20000, 20000),
      new THREE.MeshStandardMaterial({ color: 0x1a1d22, roughness: 1, metalness: 0 })
    );
    ground.rotation.x = -Math.PI / 2;
    ground.position.y = -0.4;
    scene.add(ground);

    var grid = new THREE.GridHelper(1400, 56, 0x3a4048, 0x3a4048);
    grid.material.opacity = 0.18;
    grid.material.transparent = true;
    scene.add(grid);

    var pad = new THREE.Mesh(
      new THREE.CircleGeometry(43, 64),
      new THREE.MeshStandardMaterial({ color: 0x22262c, roughness: 0.95 })
    );
    pad.rotation.x = -Math.PI / 2;
    pad.position.y = 0.05;
    scene.add(pad);

    var padRing = new THREE.Mesh(
      new THREE.RingGeometry(41, 43, 64),
      new THREE.MeshBasicMaterial({ color: 0xff7a2a, side: THREE.DoubleSide, transparent: true, opacity: 0.85 })
    );
    padRing.rotation.x = -Math.PI / 2;
    padRing.position.y = 0.08;
    scene.add(padRing);
  }

  // Placeholder rocket -- primitives standing in for a real model, same
  // approach as the SLAM and Hybrid A* sims on this site. Body, soot band,
  // interstage, fins and legs are static (no gimbal/leg-deploy animation
  // yet); the nozzle is its own group so a future gimbal rotation only
  // moves the engine, not the airframe.
  function buildRocket() {
    rocket = new THREE.Group();
    scene.add(rocket);
    var placeholder = new THREE.Group();
    rocket.add(placeholder);

    var white = new THREE.MeshStandardMaterial({ color: 0xdedad0, roughness: 0.55, metalness: 0.15 });
    var dark = new THREE.MeshStandardMaterial({ color: 0x24231f, roughness: 0.7, metalness: 0.3 });
    var soot = new THREE.MeshStandardMaterial({ color: 0x151412, roughness: 0.9 });

    var bodyH = VEH.L - 6;
    var body = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.CylinderGeometry(VEH.R, VEH.R, bodyH, 36), white);
    body.position.y = bodyH / 2 - VEH.arm + 3;
    placeholder.add(body);

    var sootBand = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.CylinderGeometry(VEH.R * 1.005, VEH.R * 1.005, 13, 36, 1, true), soot);
    sootBand.position.y = -VEH.arm + 8;
    placeholder.add(sootBand);

    var inter = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.CylinderGeometry(VEH.R, VEH.R, 3.2, 36), dark);
    inter.position.y = bodyH - VEH.arm + 4.6;
    placeholder.add(inter);

    for (var i = 0; i < 4; i++) {
      var fin = new THREE.Group();
      var blade = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.BoxGeometry(0.35, 3.6, 2.6), dark);
      blade.position.set(VEH.R + 1.3, 0, 0);
      fin.add(blade);
      fin.position.y = bodyH - VEH.arm - 0.5;
      fin.rotation.y = (i * Math.PI) / 2;
      placeholder.add(fin);
    }

    var bay = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.CylinderGeometry(VEH.R, VEH.R * 0.86, 3.4, 36), dark);
    bay.position.y = -VEH.arm + 1.5;
    placeholder.add(bay);

    for (var j = 0; j < 4; j++) {
      var pivot = new THREE.Group();
      pivot.position.set(0, -VEH.arm + 2.2, 0);
      pivot.rotation.y = (j * Math.PI) / 2 + Math.PI / 4;
      var leg = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.CylinderGeometry(0.36, 0.5, 11, 10), dark);
      leg.position.set(0, -5.5, 0);
      pivot.add(leg);
      var foot = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.CylinderGeometry(1.3, 1.3, 0.5, 12), soot);
      foot.position.set(0, -11, 0);
      pivot.add(foot);
      placeholder.add(pivot);
    }

    nozzleGroup = new THREE.Group();
    nozzleGroup.position.y = -VEH.arm;
    rocket.add(nozzleGroup);
    var bell = new THREE.Mesh(
      new THREE.CylinderGeometry(0.85, 1.55, 3.6, 24, 1, true),
      new THREE.MeshStandardMaterial({ color: 0x2a2723, roughness: 0.5, metalness: 0.6, side: THREE.DoubleSide })
    );
    bell.position.y = -1.6;
    nozzleGroup.add(bell);

    flame = new THREE.Mesh(
      new THREE.ConeGeometry(1.5, 20, 20, 1, true),
      new THREE.MeshBasicMaterial({ color: 0xff7a25, transparent: true, opacity: 0, blending: THREE.AdditiveBlending, depthWrite: false, side: THREE.DoubleSide })
    );
    flame.rotation.x = Math.PI;
    flame.position.y = -13;
    nozzleGroup.add(flame);

    flameInner = new THREE.Mesh(
      new THREE.ConeGeometry(0.7, 11, 16, 1, true),
      new THREE.MeshBasicMaterial({ color: 0xfff0d0, transparent: true, opacity: 0, blending: THREE.AdditiveBlending, depthWrite: false, side: THREE.DoubleSide })
    );
    flameInner.rotation.x = Math.PI;
    flameInner.position.y = -8;
    nozzleGroup.add(flameInner);

    glow = new THREE.PointLight(0xff7a2a, 0, 60, 2);
    glow.position.y = -3;
    nozzleGroup.add(glow);

    // Position is driven every frame from `state.alt` in tick() -- see
    // `restY` above for the rest-on-pad offset this is built around.
    rocket.position.set(0, restY + state.alt, 0);
  }

  function updateCamera(dt) {
    if (!reducedMotion) camRig.spin += dt * 0.05;
    var theta = camRig.theta + camRig.spin * 0.4;
    var phi = camRig.phi;
    // Pull back as altitude climbs so a 900 m fall still frames the
    // vehicle, and close in for the last few metres of a landing.
    var dist = clamp(110 + Math.max(state.alt, 0) * 0.22, 110, 900);
    camTarget.lerp(rocket.position, 1 - Math.pow(0.001, Math.min(dt, 0.1)));
    camera.position.set(
      camTarget.x + dist * Math.cos(phi) * Math.cos(theta),
      camTarget.y + dist * Math.sin(phi),
      camTarget.z + dist * Math.cos(phi) * Math.sin(theta)
    );
    camera.lookAt(camTarget);
  }

  function clamp(v, a, b) { return v < a ? a : v > b ? b : v; }

  function throttleFraction() {
    var pct = throttleInput ? Number(throttleInput.value) : 0;
    return pct <= 0 ? 0 : Math.max(pct / 100, VEH.minThr);
  }

  function stepPhysics(dt) {
    if (state.phase !== "falling") return;
    var thrustFrac = state.fuel > 0 ? throttleFraction() : 0;
    var thrust = thrustFrac * VEH.maxT;
    var mass = VEH.dry + state.fuel;
    var rho = PHYS.rho0 * Math.exp(-Math.max(state.alt, 0) / PHYS.H);
    var drag = 0.5 * rho * PHYS.Cd * PHYS.A * state.vy * Math.abs(state.vy);
    var accel = thrust / mass - PHYS.g0 - drag / mass;

    state.vy += accel * dt;
    state.alt += state.vy * dt;
    if (thrust > 0) state.fuel = Math.max(0, state.fuel - (thrust / (PHYS.isp * PHYS.g0)) * dt);

    if (state.alt <= 0) {
      state.alt = 0;
      state.phase = Math.abs(state.vy) <= PHYS.vTouch ? "landed" : "crashed";
      state.vy = 0;
      if (throttleInput) throttleInput.value = "0";
      updateThrottleVisual();
    }
  }

  var PHASE_LABEL = { falling: "Falling", landed: "Landed", crashed: "Crashed" };

  function updateHud() {
    if (altEl) altEl.textContent = Math.round(state.alt);
    if (vspeedEl) vspeedEl.textContent = state.vy.toFixed(1);
    if (fuelEl) fuelEl.textContent = Math.round((state.fuel / VEH.fuel0) * 100);
    if (phaseEl) {
      phaseEl.textContent = PHASE_LABEL[state.phase];
      phaseEl.classList.toggle("tone-ok", state.phase === "landed");
      phaseEl.classList.toggle("tone-bad", state.phase === "crashed");
    }
  }

  function resetRun() {
    state.alt = 900;
    state.vy = -85;
    state.fuel = VEH.fuel0;
    state.phase = "falling";
    if (throttleInput) throttleInput.value = "0";
    updateThrottleVisual();
  }

  function resize() {
    var w = canvas.clientWidth, h = canvas.clientHeight;
    if (!w || !h) return;
    renderer.setSize(w, h, false);
    camera.aspect = w / h;
    camera.updateProjectionMatrix();
  }

  buildLights();
  buildGround();
  buildRocket();
  resize();
  if (window.ResizeObserver) new ResizeObserver(resize).observe(canvas);
  else window.addEventListener("resize", resize);

  function updateThrottleVisual() {
    var pct = throttleInput ? Number(throttleInput.value) : 0;
    var t = throttleFraction(); // post-floor fraction -- see VEH.minThr
    if (throttleLabel) throttleLabel.textContent = pct + "%";
    if (flame) {
      flame.material.opacity = t > 0 ? 0.3 + t * 0.35 : 0;
      flame.scale.set(0.5 + t * 0.6, 0.4 + t * 0.9, 0.5 + t * 0.6);
    }
    if (flameInner) {
      flameInner.material.opacity = t > 0 ? 0.55 + t * 0.4 : 0;
      flameInner.scale.set(0.5 + t * 0.6, 0.4 + t * 0.9, 0.5 + t * 0.6);
    }
    if (glow) glow.intensity = t * 3.5;
  }

  var clock = new THREE.Clock();
  function tick() {
    requestAnimationFrame(tick);
    var dt = Math.min(clock.getDelta(), 0.1);
    // Fixed-step physics regardless of frame rate, then everything else
    // reads the resulting state.
    var remaining = dt;
    while (remaining > 0) {
      var h = Math.min(PHYS.step, remaining);
      stepPhysics(h);
      remaining -= h;
    }
    rocket.position.set(0, restY + state.alt, 0);
    updateCamera(dt);
    updateThrottleVisual();
    updateHud();
    renderer.render(scene, camera);
  }

  if (throttleInput) throttleInput.addEventListener("input", updateThrottleVisual);
  if (resetBtn) resetBtn.addEventListener("click", resetRun);
  updateThrottleVisual();
  updateHud();
  tick();

  // Throttle is wired to the flame/glow only so far -- moving the slider
  // lights the engine visually, with nothing pushing the vehicle yet.
  // Gravity/thrust/drag integration is the next increment.
  var throttleInput = document.getElementById("throttle");
  var throttleLabel = document.getElementById("throttleV");
  var resetBtn = document.getElementById("reset");

  function updateThrottleVisual(pct) {
    var t = pct / 100;
    if (throttleLabel) throttleLabel.textContent = pct + "%";
    if (flame) {
      flame.material.opacity = t > 0 ? 0.3 + t * 0.35 : 0;
      flame.scale.set(0.5 + t * 0.6, 0.4 + t * 0.9, 0.5 + t * 0.6);
    }
    if (flameInner) {
      flameInner.material.opacity = t > 0 ? 0.55 + t * 0.4 : 0;
      flameInner.scale.set(0.5 + t * 0.6, 0.4 + t * 0.9, 0.5 + t * 0.6);
    }
    if (glow) glow.intensity = t * 3.5;
  }

  if (throttleInput) {
    throttleInput.addEventListener("input", function () {
      updateThrottleVisual(Number(throttleInput.value));
    });
    updateThrottleVisual(Number(throttleInput.value));
  }
  if (resetBtn) {
    resetBtn.addEventListener("click", function () {
      if (throttleInput) throttleInput.value = "0";
      updateThrottleVisual(0);
    });
  }

  var tabs = document.querySelectorAll(".rl-tabbtn");
  var panels = document.querySelectorAll(".rl-tab");
  tabs.forEach(function (btn) {
    btn.addEventListener("click", function () {
      var target = btn.dataset.tab;
      tabs.forEach(function (b) { b.classList.toggle("active", b === btn); });
      panels.forEach(function (p) { p.hidden = p.dataset.tab !== target; });
    });
  });
</script>

